{"term":"wrijven","lang":"nl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"masturbation","concept":"masturbation","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":25,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To rub; used as a sexual euphemism for masturbation or mutual genital rubbing.","english_term":"to rub (sexually)","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Literal meaning is to rub; as a sexual euphemism covers both masturbation and frottage; low crude score because the base word is neutral and context carries all the sexual charge."}
{"term":"versieren","lang":"nl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"seduction","concept":"sexual_advance","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":20,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":95,"recognition_pct_peak":95,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To pick up or seduce; to successfully court or flirt someone into a romantic or sexual encounter.","english_term":"to pick up","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Literally means to decorate or adorn; the seduction sense is fully established and not considered vulgar; British English 'to chat up' is the closest functional equivalent."}
{"term":"versierpoging","lang":"nl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"seduction","concept":"sexual_advance","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":15,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":85,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A pickup attempt; the act of trying to flirt or seduce someone into a romantic or sexual encounter.","english_term":"pickup attempt","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Compound of versieren (to pick up) and poging (attempt); neutral enough for ordinary conversation; implies the attempt without guaranteeing success."}
{"term":"inpakken","lang":"nl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"seduction_success","concept":"intercourse_conquest","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":20,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To reel in or wrap up; a colloquial euphemism for successfully seducing or hooking someone for sex, framing the outcome as a competitive achievement.","english_term":"to reel someone in","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Literal meaning is to pack up or wrap; the sexual sense implies fully winning the target over; the conquest framing is embedded in the metaphor of packaging prey."}
{"term":"de deur uitgaan met","lang":"nl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"dating","concept":"sexual_relationship","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":5,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":85,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To go out with someone; a mild euphemism describing a romantic or sexual relationship by referencing its social rituals rather than any physical act.","english_term":"to go out with","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Literally to go out the door with; among the most indirect Dutch ways to acknowledge an intimate involvement; completely avoids naming any sexual act and is therefore maximally socially safe."}
{"term":"bietenbrug","lang":"nl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"romantic_rejection","concept":"sexual_advance","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":10,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":65,"recognition_pct_peak":65,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Beet bridge; used in the fixed idiom 'op de bietenbrug sturen' meaning to reject or dismiss someone after a failed romantic or sexual advance.","english_term":"to give someone the brush-off","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Always appears in idiom form 'op de bietenbrug sturen'; mapped to sexual_advance as nearest concept since bietenbrug denotes the rejection outcome of such an attempt; etymology of bietenbrug itself is obscure and contested."}
{"term":"de kers op de taart","lang":"nl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"intercourse_culminating","concept":"intercourse_complete","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":20,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The cherry on the cake; a sexual euphemism for penetrative intercourse as the culminating final act of a sexual encounter.","english_term":"the cherry on top","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Primarily a general Dutch idiom for the finishing touch; the sexual reading is context-dependent; kers carries faint virginity connotations in some registers but this phrase is not defloration-specific."}
{"term":"de kat bij de melk","lang":"nl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"sexual_temptation","concept":"lust","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":10,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The cat by the milk; an idiom invoking sexual temptation as irresistible as a cat's attraction to cream, used to describe placing someone in a compromising erotic situation.","english_term":"to put temptation in someone's way","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Typically used in constructions like 'je kunt de kat niet bij de melk zetten'; the sexual application is implicit and context-dependent; the idiom is non-sexual in many uses."}
{"term":"het haantje spelen","lang":"nl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"virility_display","concept":"womanizer","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":25,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To play the little rooster; to strut and perform masculine sexual dominance, typically while pursuing or showing off to multiple women.","english_term":"to play the stud","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Haantje is the diminutive of haan (rooster/cock); the rooster-as-sexual-male metaphor is consistent across many European languages; implies posturing rather than confirmed seduction."}
{"term":"miereneuken","lang":"nl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"intercourse_metaphor","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":60,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally to fuck ants; in overwhelming everyday use this means to nitpick or be pedantically obsessive, with neuken (to fuck) functioning as a vivid intensifier rather than a literal descriptor.","english_term":"to nitpick","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Contains neuken (crude ~85); the sexual literal meaning is vestigial in modern use; a reversed euphemism in which a crude sexual base launders an abstract concept of pedantry; listed here for the literal sexual component embedded in the word."}
{"term":"de broek laten zakken","lang":"nl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"undressing_for_sex","concept":"intercourse_implied","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":30,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":85,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To let the trousers drop; a euphemism for undressing and making oneself sexually available, or for passively submitting to sexual activity.","english_term":"to drop one's trousers","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Also used non-sexually to mean to back down or capitulate; the sexual reading implies consenting to or enabling sex by removing a clothing barrier; the passive construction suggests yielding rather than initiating."}
{"term":"zijn broek uittrekken","lang":"nl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"undressing_for_sex","concept":"intercourse_implied","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":30,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":85,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To take off one's trousers; a euphemism for disrobing as a deliberate prelude to sexual activity.","english_term":"to take one's trousers off","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"More agentive than 'de broek laten zakken'; implies active self-initiation of sexual readiness rather than passive submission; the distinction is subtle but consistent with Dutch speaker intuition."}
{"term":"ontucht","lang":"nl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"sexual_offense","concept":"indecent_act","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":20,"technical":50,"recognition_pct_current":75,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Sexual misconduct or lewdness; the formal Dutch legal and moral term for any sexual act violating decency norms, still active in criminal law especially regarding offenses against minors.","english_term":"sexual misconduct","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Remains in the Dutch Penal Code ('ontucht plegen met een minderjarige'); its archaic moral charge coexists with active legal use; broader than English 'indecent assault' and narrower than 'debauchery'."}
{"term":"hoererij","lang":"nl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"prostitution","concept":"sex_work_industry","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":55,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":55,"recognition_pct_peak":75,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Whoredom; an archaic morally charged Dutch term for prostitution or widespread sexual promiscuity, carrying strong Calvinist condemnation.","english_term":"whoredom","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Derived from hoer (whore); largely displaced in modern Dutch by prostitutie and sekswerk; still encountered in older Bible editions, historical texts, and heated moralizing rhetoric."}
{"term":"onkuisheid","lang":"nl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"unchastity","concept":"licentiousness","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":20,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":50,"recognition_pct_peak":75,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Unchastity; the state of being sexually impure or morally undisciplined, primarily a religious category denoting failure to govern carnal desire.","english_term":"unchastity","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Key term in Dutch Calvinist and Catholic moral catechesis; rarely heard outside religious or historical contexts in contemporary Dutch; the positive antonym is kuisheid (chastity)."}
{"term":"onzedelijkheid","lang":"nl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"sexual_immorality","concept":"indecency","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":15,"technical":30,"recognition_pct_current":55,"recognition_pct_peak":70,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Sexual immorality or indecency; a formal archaic term for behavior violating public moral norms, particularly in the sexual domain.","english_term":"indecency","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Broader than ontucht in that it encompasses moral violations not necessarily criminal; extensively used in 19th- and early-20th-century Dutch public discourse on morality reform."}
{"term":"wellust","lang":"nl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"lust","concept":"lust","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":25,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":65,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Voluptuousness or lust; intense carnal desire and pleasure in the body, especially as a literary and theological concept contrasted with spiritual virtue.","english_term":"lust","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Literally well-pleasure from wel (well) and lust (desire/pleasure); semantically richer than English lust since it encompasses both the desire and the pleasure of satisfying it; a primary category in Calvinist sin taxonomy."}
{"term":"vleesgenot","lang":"nl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"carnal_pleasure","concept":"carnal_desire","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":20,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":35,"recognition_pct_peak":60,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The pleasure of the flesh; carnal enjoyment of physical and sexual sensation, typically contrasted with spiritual virtue in religious writing.","english_term":"carnal pleasure","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Compound of vlees (flesh/meat) and genot (pleasure/enjoyment); strongly archaic; found mainly in historical theological texts and older literary prose; the meat-as-body metaphor carries the same spiritual-physical opposition as Latin caro."}
{"term":"vleselijk","lang":"nl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"carnal","concept":"carnal_desire","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":15,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":45,"recognition_pct_peak":65,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Carnal or fleshly; an archaic adjective meaning of or pertaining to the desires of the body, especially sexual appetite, as opposed to the spirit.","english_term":"carnal","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"From vlees (flesh); Dutch morphophonology yields vleselijk not vleeselijk; typically appears in fixed phrases such as vleselijke lusten (carnal lusts); direct calque of Latin carnalis."}
{"term":"vleeslusten","lang":"nl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"carnal_desire","concept":"concupiscence","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":20,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":35,"recognition_pct_peak":65,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The lusts of the flesh; carnal desires regarded in Dutch religious tradition as sinful drives of the body warring against the spirit.","english_term":"lusts of the flesh","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Plural compound of vlees (flesh) and lusten (lusts/desires); a central phrase in the Statenvertaling (Dutch Authorized Version) Bible and Calvinist sermon tradition; English theological cognate is concupiscence."}
{"term":"onteerden","lang":"nl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"defloration_rape","concept":"ravishment","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":35,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":40,"recognition_pct_peak":65,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To dishonor; an archaic Dutch verb meaning to sexually violate or deflower a woman, stripping her of social honor, with strong implication of rape or coercive defloration.","english_term":"to ravish","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"From eer (honor) with negating prefix ont-; the word's framing locates the harm in lost honor rather than bodily autonomy; modern Dutch uses verkrachten (to rape) instead; the honor-shame logic it encodes is now considered deeply problematic."}
{"term":"schaamdelen","lang":"nl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"organs","subcategory":"genitalia","concept":"genitalia","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":10,"technical":40,"recognition_pct_current":70,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Private parts; a dated formal Dutch term for the external genitalia, literally meaning shame parts.","english_term":"private parts","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Compound of schaam (shame/pubic) and delen (parts); the shame etymology mirrors English privates and Latin pudenda; still understood by most Dutch speakers but sounds stiff and old-fashioned in contemporary use."}
{"term":"bijslaper","lang":"nl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"extramarital_companion","concept":"extramarital_lover","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":20,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":35,"recognition_pct_peak":55,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A bed companion; an archaic Dutch term for a person who regularly shares someone's bed as an extramarital or illicit sexual partner.","english_term":"bed companion","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Compound of bij (near/alongside) and slaper (sleeper); gender-neutral in form but historically applied to both male and female concubines; compare bijvrouw for the specifically female equivalent."}
{"term":"bijvrouw","lang":"nl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"concubine","concept":"concubinage","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":15,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":30,"recognition_pct_peak":55,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A secondary wife or concubine; an archaic Dutch term for a woman who cohabits sexually with a man but holds a lower legal and social status than a legitimate wife.","english_term":"concubine","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Compound of bij (additional/secondary) and vrouw (woman/wife); appears in Dutch Bible translations (Rechters 19) and historical accounts of polygynous cultures; compare bijslaper for the gender-neutral form."}
{"term":"minnares","lang":"nl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"mistress","concept":"mistress","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":15,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":85,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A mistress or female lover; a woman engaged in an ongoing romantic and typically clandestine sexual relationship, often with a married man.","english_term":"mistress","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Derived from minnen (to love in the courtly tradition); less socially charged than maitresse; still in active modern Dutch use for an extramarital female partner; comfortable in both literary prose and tabloid journalism."}
